<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Wales under Owain Glyndwr</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1415 CE (approx.) · capital Machynlleth</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last attempt at an independent Welsh state, led by Owain Glyndwr, who was proclaimed Prince of Wales in 1400 and held parliaments at Machynlleth.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndwr</a>, a descendant of the princes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/powys-fadog">Powys Fadog</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-deheubarth">Deheubarth</a>, was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> by his followers in September 1400 after a land dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginald-grey-3rd-baron-grey-de-ruthyn">Lord Grey of Ruthin</a>. His revolt quickly spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, fed by anger at English rule and taxes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>By 1404 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Glyndwr</a> held most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberystwyth">Aberystwyth</a> castles, and summoned parliaments at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machynlleth">Machynlleth</a> and Harlech. He allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and with English rebels, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/percy-family">Percys</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-mortimer">Mortimers</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennal-letter">Pennal Letter</a> proposed an independent Welsh church and two universities.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1405 English forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry</a> recovered ground, retaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberystwyth">Aberystwyth</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a> by 1409. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Glyndwr</a> vanished around 1415. He became a lasting symbol of Welsh national identity.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-wales-under-owain-glyndwr.jpg" alt="Principality of Wales under Owain Glyndwr" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Llywelyn2000 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sycharth_home_of_Owain_Glyndwr,_Last_Prince_of_Wales_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1415 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Machynlleth</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Owain Glyndwr</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Welsh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Glyndwr's Wales, Glyndwr Rising</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1408&amp;focus=principality-of-wales-under-owain-glyndwr" aria-label="Principality of Wales under Owain Glyndwr on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4594 364 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Wales under Owain Glyndwr lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4914 620L4970 663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4914 620h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4914 620h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Machynlleth. <a href="/atlas?year=1408&amp;focus=principality-of-wales-under-owain-glyndwr">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
